<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Find the Greatest Common Factor: Mini-Lesson Review</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01547</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Find the Greatest Common Factor: Mini-Lesson Review</md:title>
    <md:uuid>ffceb9a7-35a4-4bf6-80eb-d768598e35d5</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <para id="para-00001">Which is the greatest common factor of \(48\) and \(80\)?</para>
    </section>


    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Find the Greatest Common Factor</title>
      <para id="para-00002">The greatest common factor (GCF) of two or more expressions is the largest expression that is a factor of all the expressions.</para>
      <para id="para-00003">Find the greatest common factor of \(54\) and \(36\).</para>
      <para id="para-00004"><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Factor each number into primes.</para>
      <para id="para-00005"> Factor \(54\) and \(36\).</para>
      <para id="para-00006">
          <media alt="">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_07_02_08_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <para id="para-00007">
          <media alt="">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_07_02_08_02.jpg"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <para id="para-00008"><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> List the factors. In each column, circle the common factors.</para>
      <para id="para-00009"> Circle the \(2\), \(3\), and \(3\) that are shared by both numbers.</para>
      <para id="para-00010">
          <media alt="">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_07_02_08_03.jpg"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <para id="para-00011"><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Bring down the common factors that all the expressions share.</para>
      <para id="para-00012"> Bring down the \(2\), \(3\), and \(3\).</para>
      <para id="para-00013"> GCF \(=2 \cdot 3 \cdot 3\)</para>
      <para id="para-00014"><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Multiply the factors.</para>
      <para id="para-00015"> Multiply the common factors \(2\), \(3\), and \(3\).</para>
      <para id="para-00016"> GCF \(=18\)</para>
      <para id="para-00017"> The GCF of \(54\) and \(36\) is \(18\).</para>
    </section>
<note class="try" id="note-00001">  <title>Try It: Find the Greatest Common Factor</title>
      
        <exercise id="exer-00001">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00018">Find the greatest common factor (GCF) of \(84\) and \(56\).</para>
            <para id="para-00019">Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00020">Here’s how to find the GCF.</para>
            <para id="para-00021"><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Factor each number into primes.</para>
            <para id="para-00022">   Factor \(84\) and \(56\).</para>
            <para id="para-00023">
                      <media alt="">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_07_02_08_04.jpg"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
            <para id="para-00024">
                      <media alt="">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_07_02_08_05.jpg"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
            <para id="para-00025"><emphasis>Step 2 - 
   </emphasis> List the factors. In each column, circle the common factors.</para>
            <para id="para-00026">   Circle the \(2\), \(2\), and \(7\) that are shared by both numbers.</para>
            <para id="para-00027">
                      <media alt="">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_07_02_08_06.jpg"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
            <para id="para-00028"><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Bring down the common factors that all the expressions share.</para>
            <para id="para-00029">   Bring down the \(2\), \(2\), and \(7\).</para>
            <para id="para-00030">GCF \(=227\)</para>
            <para id="para-00031"><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Multiply the factors.</para>
            <para id="para-00032">   Multiply the common factors \(2\), \(2\), and \(7\).</para>
            <para id="para-00033">   GCF \(=28\)</para>
            <para id="para-00034">   The GCF of \(84\) and \(56\) is \(28\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </note>
      <section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00003">
        <title>Check Your Understanding</title>
        <exercise id="c513f2f5-07ca-44f3-8426-5c72e0ff79ee" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <para id="para-00035">Which is the greatest common factor of \(96\) and \(72\)?</para>
            <para id="para-00036">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00037">\(24\)</para>
            <para id="para-00038">\(12\)</para>
            <para id="para-00039">\(36\)</para>
            <para id="para-00040">\(48\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00041">Correct! Check yourself: The GCF is the product of the common factors, \(2 \cdot 2 \cdot 3\).</para>
            <para id="para-00042">Try again. Take a moment to think about what you learned in the mini-lesson review.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
